WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.556
Hello!

2
00:00:00.556 --> 00:00:10.467
If a circuit without an external source admits two modes of oscillation, their sum is also a possible mode.

3
00:00:10.467 --> 00:00:16.024
This is the key property of linear equations, superposition.

4
00:00:16.024 --> 00:00:23.990
Today we state it precisely and learn to find the interval on which a solution exists.

5
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.044
Write a linear equation as L of y equals f,

6
00:00:28.044 --> 00:00:35.739
where L is the n-th derivative plus lower derivatives with coefficients depending on x.

7
00:00:35.739 --> 00:00:44.496
L is linear: acting on a combination of two functions it gives the same combination of the results.

8
00:00:44.496 --> 00:00:51.926
When f is zero the equation is called homogeneous, a different meaning of that word.

9
00:00:52.176 --> 00:01:00.042
Example: x squared y double prime minus two y equals zero, for positive x.

10
00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:07.164
x squared is a solution: two x squared minus two x squared is zero.

11
00:01:07.164 --> 00:01:16.731
One over x is a solution too: x squared times two over x cubed, minus two over x, is zero.

12
00:01:16.731 --> 00:01:24.278
By superposition, C one x squared plus C two over x is also a solution.

13
00:01:24.528 --> 00:01:30.733
Now impose y of one equals one and y prime of one equals five.

14
00:01:30.733 --> 00:01:37.438
C one plus C two equals one, and two C one minus C two equals five.

15
00:01:37.438 --> 00:01:44.544
Adding, three C one equals six, so C one is two and C two is minus one.

16
00:01:44.544 --> 00:01:49.348
Answer: y equals two x squared minus one over x.

17
00:01:49.348 --> 00:01:56.654
The determinant of the system is minus three, the value of the Wronskian.

18
00:01:56.904 --> 00:02:04.834
A key result for linear equations: if the coefficients and the right side are continuous on an interval,

19
00:02:04.834 --> 00:02:10.857
the solution of the Cauchy problem exists and is unique on that whole interval.

20
00:02:10.857 --> 00:02:16.957
So first divide by the leading coefficient and find the points of discontinuity.

21
00:02:16.957 --> 00:02:22.065
For instance, with x squared minus four in front of y double prime,

22
00:02:22.065 --> 00:02:26.030
the dangerous points are x equals two and minus two.

23
00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:35.340
The graph shows the two fundamental solutions, x squared and one over x, and the solution we found,

24
00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.177
two x squared minus one over x.

25
00:02:38.177 --> 00:02:48.518
The third curve is a linear combination of the first two; at x equals one its value is one and its slope is five.

26
00:02:48.768 --> 00:02:54.169
Check yourself: for which equations does superposition hold?

27
00:02:54.169 --> 00:02:57.950
Choose an answer and read the explanation.
